          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>In monogenic obesity, single-gene mutations disrupt a specific hypothalamic circuit that controls appetite. Which pathway is primarily affected?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Hypothalamic-pituitary-thyroid axis</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The HPT axis regulates metabolism through thyroid hormones. While hypothyroidism causes weight gain, it is a secondary cause of obesity — not the pathway disrupted in monogenic forms.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Leptin-melanocortin pathway</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. The leptin-melanocortin pathway in the hypothalamus is the main circuit controlling appetite. Mutations at key points — including the leptin receptor, POMC, and MC4R (the commonest) — produce severe, early-onset obesity.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The RAAS regulates blood pressure and fluid balance, not appetite. Monogenic obesity results from disruption of the leptin-melanocortin pathway, the hypothalamic circuit controlling appetite.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Dopamine reward pathway</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>While dopamine influences food reward and eating behaviour, monogenic obesity specifically involves mutations disrupting the leptin-melanocortin pathway — not the dopamine system.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Monogenic obesity results from single-gene mutations disrupting the leptin-melanocortin pathway — the hypothalamic circuit controlling appetite. Key mutation sites include LEPR, POMC, and MC4R (the commonest)."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Exactly. The leptin-melanocortin pathway is the hypothalamic appetite-regulation circuit. MC4R mutations are the commonest single-gene disruption of this pathway."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
