          "label": "<p>At what BMI threshold should secondary prevention measures, including brief life</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>At what BMI threshold should secondary prevention measures, including brief lifestyle counselling, be initiated for an Indian patient?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>BMI ≥ 25 kg/m²</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>This is the global WHO cut-off for overweight, but it underestimates risk in South Asians. Indian guidelines set the overweight threshold at BMI ≥23 kg/m², where secondary prevention should begin.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>BMI ≥ 30 kg/m²</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>A BMI of 30 is the Western obesity threshold. For Indian patients, metabolic risk appears much earlier. Secondary prevention should start at BMI ≥23 kg/m².</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>BMI ≥ 23 kg/m²</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. The Indian overweight threshold is BMI ≥23 kg/m² — below the global cut-off of 25. Brief lifestyle counselling at this stage can prevent the progression to obesity and its complications.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>BMI ≥ 27.5 kg/m²</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>BMI 27.5 is the Indian threshold for pharmacotherapy (with a comorbidity), not for secondary prevention screening. Lifestyle counselling should begin earlier, at BMI ≥23 kg/m².</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Secondary prevention in Indian patients starts at BMI ≥23 kg/m² — lower than the global threshold of 25. South Asians carry higher metabolic risk at lower body weights, making earlier intervention essential."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. Early intervention at BMI ≥23 in Indian patients catches metabolic risk before complications develop — a key advantage of population-specific thresholds."
                }
              ],
